== 2.10 Chapter 2 Practice Drill — 40 Questions

40 questions graded in the order the CBT engine is likely to meet them. Allow 44 minutes; the key and a full solution for every question follow.

#mcq(1, [The breakdown of rock in place by heat, water and living things is called],
  [weathering],
  [deposition],
  [leaching],
  [erosion]
)

#mcq(2, [Rock formed by the cooling and solidifying of magma is],
  [sedimentary],
  [metamorphic],
  [igneous],
  [residual]
)

#mcq(3, [Which of these is a sedimentary rock?],
  [basalt],
  [limestone],
  [gneiss],
  [granite]
)

#mcq(4, [Marble is formed when limestone is subjected to],
  [deposition under water],
  [cooling from a melt],
  [solution in acid rain],
  [heat and pressure]
)

#mcq(5, [Which rock type is most likely to contain fossils?],
  [sedimentary],
  [metamorphic],
  [igneous],
  [volcanic]
)

#mcq(6, [Soil formed over granite in a wet climate is likely to be],
  [alkaline and heavy],
  [saline and waterlogged],
  [acidic and sandy],
  [neutral and shallow]
)

#mcq(7, [The horizon that consists of fresh and partly decayed litter is the],
  [C horizon],
  [O horizon],
  [B horizon],
  [A horizon]
)

#mcq(8, [The layer in which most of the humus is mixed with mineral soil is the],
  [C horizon],
  [A horizon],
  [R horizon],
  [B horizon]
)

#mcq(9, [Material washed down from the topsoil accumulates in the],
  [R horizon],
  [B horizon],
  [O horizon],
  [A horizon]
)

#mcq(10, [The proportion of sand, silt and clay in a soil is its],
  [texture],
  [consistence],
  [structure],
  [profile]
)

#mcq(11, [The arrangement of soil particles into crumbs, blocks or plates is called],
  [permeability],
  [structure],
  [texture],
  [porosity]
)

#mcq(12, [A soil with a very high clay content is likely to],
  [hold no nutrients at all],
  [drain too fast and dry out],
  [drain slowly and waterlog easily],
  [be free of roots]
)

#mcq(13, [Which soil fraction holds water and nutrients most strongly?],
  [gravel],
  [sand],
  [coarse silt],
  [clay]
)

#mcq(14, [The best soil for most crops is a],
  [gravel],
  [loam],
  [pure sand],
  [heavy clay]
)

#mcq(15, [The five factors of soil formation are parent material, climate, organisms, relief and],
  [depth],
  [time],
  [texture],
  [colour]
)

#mcq(16, [The climate factor in soil formation works mainly through],
  [rainfall and temperature, which drive weathering and leaching],
  [the depth of the bedrock],
  [wind direction only],
  [soil colour]
)

#mcq(17, [The removal of dissolved nutrients from the topsoil into the subsoil is],
  [leaching],
  [deposition],
  [weathering],
  [erosion]
)

#mcq(18, [Soils on steep slopes are usually shallow because],
  [rainfall is lower on slopes],
  [roots cannot grow],
  [the rock is always harder],
  [the eroded material moves downhill and does not accumulate]
)

#mcq(19, [Soil pH measures],
  [water content],
  [particle size],
  [acidity and alkalinity],
  [organic matter]
)

#mcq(20, [Most crops grow best in a soil with a pH of about],
  [3.0 to 4.0],
  [8.5 to 9.5],
  [5.5 to 7.0],
  [1.0 to 2.0]
)

#mcq(21, [A soil with a pH of 4.5 is best corrected by applying],
  [urea],
  [sulphur],
  [ammonium sulphate],
  [lime]
)

#mcq(22, [In Nigeria, soils of the wet south are generally more acidic than those of the north because],
  [heavy rain leaches the bases out],
  [the south is colder],
  [the north has more clay],
  [the south has more limestone]
)

#mcq(23, [Alluvial soils are found],
  [on the plateau],
  [on river floodplains],
  [in the mangrove swamp only],
  [on hilltops]
)

#mcq(24, [The soils of the mangrove swamp are difficult for agriculture mainly because they are],
  [too acidic to support life],
  [entirely rocky],
  [saline and waterlogged],
  [too dry]
)

#mcq(25, [Laterite soils are characterised by],
  [high humus throughout],
  [loose sandy texture],
  [a neutral pH],
  [iron enrichment and hardening when exposed]
)

#mcq(26, [Field capacity is the water],
  [held so tightly that roots cannot take it],
  [added by irrigation],
  [lost to the atmosphere],
  [left in the soil after excess has drained]
)

#mcq(27, [A plant wilts although the soil is still damp when the moisture has reached],
  [field capacity],
  [the water table],
  [saturation],
  [the wilting point]
)

#mcq(28, [The water available to a crop is the difference between],
  [field capacity and the wilting point],
  [the water table and the surface],
  [rainfall and evaporation],
  [saturation and field capacity]
)

#mcq(29, [Soil air is important to a crop because roots need it for],
  [germination only],
  [photosynthesis],
  [respiration],
  [transpiration only]
)

#mcq(30, [Humus improves a soil by],
  [reducing microbial life],
  [holding water and nutrients and improving structure],
  [raising the pH to 9],
  [increasing the sand fraction]
)

#mcq(31, [A crumb structure is desirable because it],
  [allows both drainage and aeration while holding moisture],
  [blocks root growth],
  [compacts the soil],
  [prevents water entry]
)

#mcq(32, [Which of these organisms is most important in mixing organic matter into the topsoil?],
  [whitefly],
  [earthworm],
  [grasshopper],
  [quelea bird]
)

#mcq(33, [A hard layer formed just below plough depth by repeated ploughing is called],
  [a rill],
  [a laterite cap only],
  [a plough pan],
  [an O horizon]
)

#mcq(34, [Soil colour is used as an indicator of],
  [organic matter, iron and drainage],
  [texture alone],
  [depth alone],
  [pH alone]
)

#mcq(35, [Chemical weathering is fastest in a climate that is],
  [hot and wet],
  [polar],
  [cold and dry],
  [temperate and dry]
)

#mcq(36, [Which process turns granite into the clay minerals of a soil?],
  [abrasion by wind],
  [frost shattering],
  [deposition],
  [hydrolysis]
)

#mcq(37, [Which of these is a biological agent of weathering?],
  [tree roots prising rock apart],
  [frost shattering],
  [wind abrasion],
  [carbonation by rain]
)

#mcq(38, [The depth of the A horizon is what a farmer means by],
  [soil colour],
  [deep or shallow soil],
  [field capacity],
  [soil pH]
)

#mcq(39, [Which practice would most directly improve the structure of a compacted clay soil?],
  [ploughing deeper every year],
  [applying more nitrogen],
  [adding organic matter and avoiding work when wet],
  [removing all the topsoil]
)

#mcq(40, [A soil that crusts hard after rain and cracks when dry is most likely],
  [a sandy soil],
  [a gravelly soil],
  [a loam],
  [a clay soil with poor structure]
)

